{"records":[{"id":"ycnm3f8gsf","title":"Reconstitution of C9orf72 GGGGCC repeat-associated non-AUG translation with purified human translation factors","description":"Data in ''Reconstitution of C9orf72 GGGGCC repeat-associated non-AUG translation with purified human translation factors\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-08-31T14:22:46.543Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ycnm3f8gsf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ycnm3f8gsf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Hayato","last_name":"Ito","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Luminescence","Western Blot","Gene Translation","In Vitro Study"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"332kvzz8xk","title":"RNA-seq of a small molecule targeted recruitment of a ribonuclease to degrade the c9ALS/FTD r(G4C2) repeat expansion ","description":"We are publishing a study of a small molecule targeted recruitment of a ribonuclease to degrade the c9ALS/FTD r(G4C2) repeat expansion. We performed multiple RNA-seq experiments to evaluate the effect of the compound on transcriptome, including the 4 datasets: SET1. RNA-seq analysis of c9ALS patient-derived iPSCs treated with 50 nM of 7 plotted as average Log2(Fold Change) vs -Log10(q-value) (n = 1 C9orf72 iPSC line, 3 replicates per line).  The amounts of mutant allele containing the repeat expansion were calculated using the coding SNPs as a proxy for abundance. SET2. RNA-seq analysis of iPSCs from healthy donors treated with 50 nM of 7 plotted as averege Log2(Fold Change) vs -Log10(q-value)  (n = 1 C9orf72 iPSC line, 3 replicates per line).  The amounts of mutant allele containing the repeat expansion were calculated using the coding SNPs as a proxy for abundance. SET3. RNA-seq analysis of +/+PWR500 mice treated with 33 nmol of 7 (n = 3 mice per treatment group).  The amounts of mutant allele containing the repeat expansion were calculated using the coding SNPs as a proxy for abundance. SET4. RNA-seq analysis of c9ALS patient-derived iPSNs treated with 50 nM of 7 plotted as average Log10(TPM) (n = 1 C9orf72 iPSN line, 3 replicates per line).   The amounts of mutant allele containing the repeat expansion were calculated using the coding SNPs as a proxy for abundance.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-08-02T14:21:43.617Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/332kvzz8xk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/332kvzz8xk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jessica","last_name":"Bush","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Haruo ","last_name":"Aikawa","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yuquan","last_name":"Tong","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hailey  ","last_name":"Olafson","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kendra","last_name":"Mckee","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Eric","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Matthew","last_name":"Disney","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["RNA Sequencing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"kc64gxd8r3","title":"Dissecting the mechanism of NOP56 GGCCUG repeat-associated non-AUG translation using cell-free translation systems.","description":"Raw data","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-05-12T19:01:55.236Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/kc64gxd8r3","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/kc64gxd8r3"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Hideki","last_name":"Taguchi","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"vvdbs49mct","title":"Canonical translation factors eIF1A and eIF5B modulate initiation step of Repeat Associated Non-AUG translation","description":"raw data","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-08-15T21:17:25.273Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vvdbs49mct","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vvdbs49mct"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Hideki","last_name":"Taguchi","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"6z6zd9gjbb","title":"RNA-seq data for a small molecule alleviating C9orf72 ALS pathology by interfacing G4C2 RNA repeats with endogenous decay pathways","description":"A hexanucleotide repeat expansion in intron 1 of the C9orf72 gene is the most common genetic cause of amyotrophic lateral sclerosis and frontotemporal dementia, or c9ALS/FTD.  The RNA transcribed from the expansion, r(G4C2)exp, causes various disease-associated pathologies, including intron retention, aberrant translation that produces toxic dipeptide repeat proteins (DPRs), and sequestration of RNA-binding proteins (RBPs) within RNA foci in a subset of cells in the central nervous system.  Identification of this RNA species as the central toxin in c9ALS/FTD has provided a potential therapeutic target.  Here, we show a repurposed antitumor drug obtained through compound derivatization potently and selectively interacts with the disease-associated r(G4C2)exp and mitigates c9ALS/FTD disease pathologies in patient-derived spinal neurons. We performed the total RNA-seq of compound-treated cells from both ALS-patients and healthy donors to support that this small molecule has limited off targets on the human transcriptome.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-10-05T06:47:30.887Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/6z6zd9gjbb","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/6z6zd9gjbb"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jessica","last_name":"Bush","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yuquan","last_name":"Tong","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Samantha","last_name":"Meyer","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Haruo","last_name":"Aikawa","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jessica","last_name":"Childs-Disney","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Matthew","last_name":"Disney","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["RNA Sequencing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9hs5d4fvgd","title":"Sexually dimorphic RNA helicases DDX3X and DDX3Y differentially regulate RNA metabolism through phase separation","description":"Sex differences are pervasive in human health and disease. One of the major keys to sex-biased differences lies in the sex chromosomes, which encode a group of sex-specific protein homologs. Although the functions of the X chromosome proteins are well appreciated, how they compare with their Y chromosome homologs remains elusive. Herein, using ensemble and single-molecule techniques, we report that the sex chromosome-encoded RNA helicases DDX3X and DDX3Y are distinct in their regulation of RNA translation and in their propensities for liquid-liquid phase separation (LLPS) and dissolution. We demonstrate that the N-terminal intrinsically disordered region of DDX3Y more strongly promotes LLPS than the corresponding region of DDX3X and that the weaker ATPase activity of DDX3Y compared to DDX3X contributes to the slower disassembly dynamics of DDX3Y-positive condensates and stress granules (SGs). Importantly, DDX3X- and DDX3Y-positive SGs have common and unique mRNA targets, and DDX3Y-dependent LLPS represses mRNA translation more strongly than DDX3X-dependent LLPS. Lastly, we show that DDX3Y enhances the aggregation of FUS, an RNA-binding protein implicated in amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, more strongly than DDX3X. Our study provides a platform for future comparisons of sex chromosome-encoded protein homologs, providing insights into sex differences in RNA metabolism and human disease.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-05-19T18:19:16.075Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9hs5d4fvgd","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9hs5d4fvgd"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Hui","last_name":"Shen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Amber","last_name":"Yanas","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michael C. ","last_name":"Owens","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Celia","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Clark","last_name":"Fritsch","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Charlotte M.","last_name":"Fare","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Katie E.","last_name":"Copley","orcid":null},{"first_name":"James","last_name":"Shorter","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yale E.","last_name":"Goldman","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kathy Fange","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["RNA","Condensation","Helicase"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gfcn2wmrpf","title":"Dataset for the paper entitled \"A genetic map of the response to DNA damage in human cells\" by Olivieri M et al. Cell 2020","description":"We used pooled CRISPR/Cas9 screens in the human RPE1-hTERT p53-/- cell line against 27 genotoxic agents. The Dataset herein make the primary data available. The files are:\n\n* Additional genes of interest (discussion and data relating to TMEM2, ESD, USP37, PHF12, BTAF1 and DRAP1)\n* Supplementary Raw Data: Data used to make all the graphs in the manuscript\n* Folder \"CRISPR screen readcount files\". Readcounts for the screens undertaken as part of this study.\n* Folder \"Raw images for immunoblots\": uncropped images for all the blots in the manuscript\n* Folder: \"Additional QC analyses of CRISPR screens\": Additional analyses that measure the quality of the screens using Presion-Recall curves of essential genes and an estimate of genes that scored as hits due to batch effects.\n* Folder \"Data_Analysis_Code\" R markdown file along with data files\n* File: \"Additional Genes of Interests\" contains a short discussion of genes we found noteworthy after analysis of our screen data.\n\nVersion 2 note: this version differs from Version 1 by a single file (FDRPos_31screens.csv) which has a tab error fixed.\n\n\n\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-07-07T14:26:40.176Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gfcn2wmrpf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gfcn2wmrpf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Daniel","last_name":"Durocher","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michele","last_name":"Olivieri","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Data Quality","Primary Data"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"835fcfbvhs","title":"Preparation of CRISPR/Cas9 and gRNA plasmids","description":"Human genome engineering has been transformed by the introduction of the CRISPR (clustered regularly interspaced short palindromic repeats)/Cas (CRISPR-associated) system found in most bacteria and archaea. Type II CRISPR/Cas systems have been engineered to induce RNA-guided genome editing in human cells, where small RNAs function together with Cas9 nucleases for sequence-specific cleavage of target sequences. Here we describe the protocol for Cas9-mediated human genome engineering, including construct building and transfection methods necessary for delivering Cas9 and guide RNA (gRNA) into human-induced pluripotent stem cells (hiPSCs) and HEK293 cells. Following genome editing, we also describe methods to assess genome editing effi- ciency using next-generation sequencing and isolate monoclonal hiPSCs with the desired modifications for downstream applications. Keywords: genome engineering • CRISPR • human stem cells","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2016-12-12T12:58:15.307Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/835fcfbvhs","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/835fcfbvhs"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"George","last_name":"Church","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Experimental Method"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"6bkjdg5g85","title":"Zmat3 is a key splicing regulator in the p53 tumor suppression program","description":"Although TP53 is the most commonly mutated gene in human cancers, the p53-dependent transcriptional programs mediating tumor suppression remain incompletely understood. Here, to uncover critical components downstream of p53 in tumor suppression, we perform unbiased RNAi and CRISPR/Cas9-based genetic screens in vivo.  These screens converge upon the p53-inducible gene Zmat3, encoding an RNA-binding-protein, and we demonstrate that ZMAT3 is an important tumor suppressor downstream of p53 in mouse KrasG12D-driven lung and liver cancers and human carcinomas. Integrative analysis of the ZMAT3 RNA-binding landscape and transcriptomic profiling reveals that ZMAT3 directly modulates exon inclusion in transcripts encoding proteins of diverse functions, including the p53 inhibitors MDM4 and MDM2, splicing regulators, and components of varied cellular processes. Interestingly, these exons are enriched in NMD signals, and, accordingly, ZMAT3 broadly affects target transcript stability. Collectively, these studies reveal ZMAT3 as a novel RNA-splicing and homeostasis regulator and key component of p53-mediated tumor suppression.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-11-06T16:30:19.918Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/6bkjdg5g85","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/6bkjdg5g85"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kathryn","last_name":"Bieging-Rolett","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","Tumor","Alternative Splicing","RNA Sequencing","P53","Mouse Model of Cancer","Adenocarcinoma","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"z4ht3txn2k","title":"Accompanying Files for paper entitled: \"Genome-scale mapping of DNA damage suppressors through phenotypic CRISPR/Cas9 screens\" - Zhao et al. Molecular Cell 2023","description":"Accompanying Files for paper entitled: \"Genome-scale mapping of DNA damage suppressors through phenotypic CRISPR/Cas9 screens\" - Zhao et al. Molecular Cell 2023. This dataset contains Source and additional Data for all figures.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-06-06T07:16:19.286Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/z4ht3txn2k","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/z4ht3txn2k"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Daniel","last_name":"Durocher","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yichao","last_name":"Zhao","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01gavpb45","name":"Canadian Institutes of Health Research","grant_id":null}],"categories":["DNA Replication","DNA Repair","Flow Cytometry","Cancer Chemotherapy","DNA Damage","Functional Genomics","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":139965,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":180,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":139965,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":139333,"restricted":67,"embargoed":565},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2333},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1777},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":663},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":476},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":451},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":437},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":421},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":383},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":309},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":278}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":137312},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1908},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":186},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":138},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":79},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":59},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":43},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":31},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":1265},{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":1109},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":516},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":407},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":362}]}}